	doi = {10.1109/ICHI69079.2026.00152},
	abstract = {Dementia affects over 57 million people worldwide, and behavioral and psychological symptoms of dementia (BPSD), including agitation, depression, and social withdrawal, are primary drivers of informal caregiver burden. Socially assistive robots (SARs) demonstrate consistent short-term benefits for emotional engagement in persons living with dementia (PLWDs), yet deployed systems share a fundamental limitation where vocal interaction is static and pre-scripted, unable to adapt to observed agitation state in real time. This paper presents Closed-loop Adaptive Response Engine (CARE), a platform-agnostic multi-agent AI framework designed to address this gap. CARE coordinates four specialized agents, an Orchestrator, a Computer Vision Perception Agent, a Large Language Model (LLM)-based Conversational Agent, and a Vocal Interaction Agent in a continuous pipeline where vision-derived agitation scores drive adaptive vocal responses. We propose a within-subject feasibility study comparing adaptive and fixed vocal interaction in adults with mild-to-moderate dementia.},
